# Project rules — Men's Kegel App

## Product

A pelvic floor trainer for adult men (18+).
Temporary display name: "Kegel Exercises" — keep it in one place so it is easy to change.
Guided sessions start within two minutes of install; day one is a real ~2-minute session.
An 8-week-plus level plan covers bladder control, erection support, ejaculation control, post-prostate-surgery recovery, and general pelvic health.
Safety and relaxation content is always free; Premium unlocks Levels 2–5 and goal-specific track content.
All training data stays on the device; there is no account in v1.
Wording and visuals stay clinical and non-sexual, including the store listing.
One Flutter codebase for Android and iOS. First store release is Android; keep all code iOS-ready (no Android-only API without a platform check). iOS submission comes after Prompt 13.
Do not hard-code prices or trial length; they come from the store products via RevenueCat.

## Plan behaviour

- "Mild discomfort" twice in a row only suggests stepping down a level. Never step down automatically.
- Check-in baseline is offered once, after the third completed session overall, then every 4 weeks.

## Principles

- First rep within 2 minutes of install.
- Ask only what changes the plan.
- Claim only what we can source. No invented statistics, testimonials, endorsement logos or predicted results.
- Relaxation and safety content is always free.
- Data stays on the device. No account in v1.
- Clinical, non-sexual wording and visuals everywhere, including the store listing.

## Stack

Do not add other packages without asking.

- Flutter stable, Dart 3
- material_ui (required: this Flutter SDK moved Material out of the framework; do not import `package:flutter/material.dart`)
- flutter_riverpod (no code generation)
- go_router
- drift + shared_preferences
- purchases_flutter (RevenueCat)
- flutter_local_notifications + timezone
- flutter_tts (on-device voice cues)
- wakelock_plus
- flutter_svg
- url_launcher
- flutter_localizations / intl
- firebase_core + firebase_analytics + firebase_crashlytics — **not in pubspec until Prompt 11** (Analytics, Crashlytics, privacy)

## Architecture

- Feature-first folders under `lib/features`; shared code in `lib/core`; app wiring in `lib/app`.
- Screens only draw; logic lives in Riverpod notifiers and plain Dart classes.
- The session engine is pure Dart with no Flutter imports.
- Levels, exercises, tracks and articles live in `assets/content` as JSON or Markdown, never hard-coded in widgets.
- All colours, spacing, radii and text styles come from `lib/app/theme/tokens.dart`.
- All user-facing strings go in the ARB file.

## Safety rules

1. Pain at onboarding or after a session moves the user to relaxation only and shows a clinician note (O3, S4, H3).
2. No exercises while a catheter is in place (O3, H1).
3. Strengthening is capped at 3 sessions a day (H1).
4. Every level has a weekly rest day and daily relaxation breathing (section 8).
5. The "relax buttocks, thighs and stomach, keep breathing" cue appears in every session (S3).
6. Stopping urine flow is described as a one-time test, never as an exercise (T1).
7. No promise of results anywhere. Timelines say "results vary" (O6, Learn).
8. A check-in that shows no change after 8 weeks suggests seeing a doctor (G2).
9. The app never diagnoses. It does not label a user as having erectile dysfunction, premature ejaculation or any other condition from their answers.

## Copy rules

- Use the exact copy in the spec where it exists.
- Never invent medical claims, statistics, testimonials or result promises.
- Clinical, non-sexual wording only.
- No emoji in the UI.

## Privacy

- Data stays on the device; no accounts.
- Analytics may only carry the events and properties in spec section 14.
- Safety answers, check-in scores and pain answers must never be sent anywhere.

## Workflow

- After every task run `flutter analyze` and `flutter test` and fix all issues.
- Keep changes small.
- If the spec is ambiguous or conflicts with these rules, stop and ask instead of guessing.
- List anything skipped at the end of the reply.

## Code style

- Small widgets, const constructors.
- No business logic in `build` methods.
- Null safety and meaningful names.
- A short doc comment on every public class.
